ith the membrane  so to summarize our results we had 529  strains in our initial screen that were  negative  we had 169 that were soluble and  positive and then 312 that were  positive at the internuclear membrane  which is what we were trying to screen  for  then we did a secondary screen with  these negative  hits the c-terminus of many proteins  has signal sequences that are  responsible for localization within the  cell  and so we wanted to counter screen those  tagged at the n-terminus  and once we did that we found a hundred  more proteins  that were positive for the inner nuclear  membrane and so we have 412  proteins that are potentially at the  internuclear membrane  and given that high number we were  somewhat skeptical of those results and  we wanted to do  strong validation of these results and  the strongest way we know how to  validate is  with immunogold electron microscopy and  so you can see here  in electron microscopy you could easily  resolve both the inner and the outer  leaflet  of the of the nuclear envelope and these  black dots  are our immunogold signals and we can  quantify what percentage of those are  present at the internuclear envelope  and you can see that some of them are  quite low but many of them have  significant numbers of  immunoglobulizations that are at the  internuclear envelope so  we were able to confirm many of these  signals  another way we validated this was with  theta nuclei theta nuclei are so named  because they look like the greek symbol  theta  and these are nuclei that have a bridge  of internuclear membrane that goes  through the middle of the nucleus  and so we don't expect any outer nuclear  membrane proteins to be on this bridge  and so if we see signal on that bridge  as you can see here  we see it here and we see it here  we were able to validate the  localization of many of our signals to  the internuclear envelope  and so um we created a tool  um a website that you can access if you  want to if you go to 