ith the membrane  so to summarize our results we had 529  strains in our initial screen that were  negative  we had 169 that were soluble and  positive and then 312 that were  positive at the internuclear membrane  which is what we were trying to screen  for  then we did a secondary screen with  these negative  hits the c-terminus of many proteins  has signal sequences that are  responsible for localization within the  cell  and so we wanted to counter screen those  tagged at the n-terminus  and once we did that we found a hundred  more proteins  that were positive for the inner nuclear  membrane and so we have 412  proteins that are potentially at the  internuclear membrane  and given that high number we were  somewhat skeptical of those results and  we wanted to do  strong validation of these results and  the strongest way we know how to  validate is  with immunogold electron microscopy and  so you can see here  in electron microscopy you could easily  resolve both the inner and the outer  leaflet  of the of the nuclear envelope and these  black dots  are our immunogold signals and we can  quantify what percentage of those are  present at the internuclear envelope  and you can see that some of them are  quite low but many of them have  significant numbers of  immunoglobulizations that are at the  internuclear envelope so  we were able to confirm many of these  signals  another way we validated this was with  theta nuclei theta nuclei are so named  because they look like the greek symbol  theta  and these are nuclei that have a bridge  of internuclear membrane that goes  through the middle of the nucleus  and so we don't expect any outer nuclear  membrane proteins to be on this bridge  and so if we see signal on that bridge  as you can see here  we see it here and we see it here  we were able to validate the  localization of many of our signals to  the internuclear envelope  and so um we created a tool  um a website that you can access if you  want to if you go to this  link you can look up your favorite  protein  and see if it was um screened in our  screen and whether or not it was at the  inner nuclear membrane  and this is a tool for the community to  use this is showing the c terminus  viewer but there's also an n-terminus  viewer as well  so the big question we had then at that  point was why are so many proteins at  the inner nuclear membrane  um and we don't really know  um one of the things may you know  obviously the most intriguing option is  that these have novel  unknown novel functions of the  internuclear envelope  like i said before we don't really know  what role diffusion in non-specific  transport plays  but in particular we are interested in  the internuclear membrane quality  control  process and so what we did was we  did a counter screen where we deleted  different pathways of different quality  control  components within the yeast cell and saw  how that affected our internuclear  membrane localization and so  the particular one we were interested in  was asi-1 which is the internuclear  membrane associated decay pathway  some of these components are shared  between the er and the inner nuclear  membrane  but we also had components that were  unique to the er  decay and pep4 is a vacuole or decay  component so um what we did was we  started a second round of screening and  now since we want to screen so many more  proteins we wanted to try to automate  things and speed them up a little bit  so we didn't commit so much time to this  um and so part of this was  we just had to eliminate some of the  more difficult strains to grow  and to image we switched our microscope  now we're using a spinning disc  microscope with an scmos  camera scientific cmos camera this is  very sensitive  and it has a wide field of view for fast  screening  but it is not as sensitive as our first  round of screening was  and so um but but it's able to see many  of the proteins that we're looking for  we're also using where this is a nikon  microscope we're using their jobs  interface for automation  and that allows us to automate many of  the the the imaging processes  and all this together allowed us to  image 96 strains in two hours  and so all together we were able to do  all of this in less than a month of  imaging time  so a considerable speed up compared to  what we did before the other thing we've  done is we've used  automated segmentation and analysis  image analysis  and that has allowed us to very rapidly  analyze these results  and do them in a quantitative fashion um  so our results fall into  uh three basic categories one is  proteins that were not  at the internuclear membrane and  suddenly are um  and this i'm showing the example here of  asi one deletion that was that in mad  component  um a more common occurrence is a protein  that was weakly  at the inner nuclear membrane and  becomes stronger in that  in that compartment and then we had some  that um  went from basically negative or weak  soluble signal  to stronger soluble signal and we're not  going to talk about those so much  so i'm not going to go into incredible  detail on these results  but i wanted to talk about just general  trends that we saw  and one of the general trends that we  saw was that this asi-1  protein deletion um results in  a much larger proportion of proteins  increasing at the internuclear membrane  compared to some of these other  categories that we have what's  interesting though is and that makes  sense with its role  as an idmat component what's interesting  though is that all these components  affect the localization of significant  numbers of of  proteins but in addition  um there's many of the proteins at the  internuclear membrane that are not  affected by any of these pathways  so a couple of conclusions we can draw  off from that just broadcast  conclusions um the inner nuclear  membrane proteome contains a large  number of proteins  we are still not sure whether these are  functional functional or accidental  components  um but these are fully formed proteins  and so  it's likely that even if there's  accidental diffusion of these proteins  into the into the inner nuclear membrane  they may be functioning in a  non-canonical fashion once they're there  um many are unaffected by degradation  pathway mutants  so we don't know if there's other  undisclosed undiscovered pathways that  are involved  or potentially these pathways work  together in compensatory ways  that we haven't anticipated yet um and  as i said  the nmad complex results in the highest  proportion of inm  increases as expected  so i wanted to spend the end of my talk  talking about  some other fun things that we're doing  with split gfp  and so i want to start with a question  that was  that came up as we were doing this  screen um so one of the things we  noticed was at the inner nuclear  membrane  was the er transocon complex  and this is involved in inserting newly  translated peptides  into the er membrane um  and this complex has two versions it has  a version with a core of 661 and a  version with core of ssh1  um and these are both at the  internuclear membrane in high  quantities what's interesting is  these proteins form complexes with sbh1  and sss1  and in this case sph2 and sss1 and  sbh2 is  at at the internuclear membrane as you  can see here  but sbh1 is not at the internuclear  membrane and so that raised the question  as to  how this complex can potentially  function at the internuclear membrane  is sbh2 somehow substituting for sbh1 in  this complex  at the internuclear membrane so in order  to investigate this  we turn to a technique called  fluorescence cross-correlation  spectroscopy  um fossils cost cost correlation  spectroscopy  measures the noise in the  co-diffusion of labeled molecules in  order to measure the extent of  co-diffusion and so  um here as an example you could park  your confocal microscope somewhere in a  cell  and if you have green and red molecules  diffusing independently of one another  you're going to see random signals  in their diffusion traces but if you  have molecules moving together you're  going to see that these signals are  highly correlated and that allows you to  measure the extent of interaction of  these  of these proteins on the membrane we  have to do a little trick  we scan repeatedly as a function of time  and that allows us to track  the membrane here's those scans in a  kymograph form  we can track the membrane while we watch  molecules diffuse and  fluctuate on that membrane  okay so getting back to our 661 ssh1  complex  um we started by looking at the cortical  er  where we expect these to be behaving in  their canonical state  and as expected 661 has a has a high  affinity has a low  kd for sph-1  at the cortical er um conversely  um ssh1 has a low kd or a high affinity  for sph2 as expected  and when we go to the internuclear  membrane the affinity of  both of these components for sbh2 the  only component that's at the  internuclear membrane  both of these have a very high affinity  for that component  indicating that it's possible quite  possibly substituting in  where the other component is not  available at the inner nuclear membrane  another fun thing that we've been able  to do with split gfp is we've been able  to utilize it in super resolution  studies to look at  the geometry of different complexes one  example of that  is our investigations into the sun cache  complex  the sun cache proteins are known to span  the nuclear envelope  and act as as tethers for the for the  nuclear envelope and in yeast the  proteins that  do this function are mps3 and mps2  and these proteins also are involved in  stabilizing the centrosomal pore in the  nuclear envelope yeast has  a centrosome that sits in the nuclear  envelope called the spindlple body  and we wanted to understand the geometry  of this interaction and we had a  hypothesis  that um this this interaction at the c  terminus could be  added to an interaction at the end  terminus that could possibly stabilize  the curvature  of the membrane at the nuclear envelope  and so  we did super resolution um with this  this split gfp  where we put the split gfp either at  both c termini  of mps2 and mps3 or at both n termini  and we get complementation in both cases  um confirming the possibility of this um  [Music]  of this parallel um complex forming here  um the other thing that was interesting  is we also see complementation of this  at the duplicated centrosome and this  duplicated centrosome hasn't inserted  into the membrane yet  and this suggests that potentially mps2  and mps3 are involved  at the duplicated centrosome starting to  remodel the membrane in preparation for  insertion  of this new centrosome that's an  intriguing possibility that we want to  pursue further  so to conclude um gfp complementation  assays enabled our proteome screen  we saw a very large number of  transmembrane proteins at the  internuclear membrane and are still  investigating that  the quality control pathways influence  some of those localizations  and nmad clearly has a large role but  there's many also that aren't  involved we were able to use gfp  complementation for fluctuation  microscopy  spectroscopy um of internuclear membrane  interactions  um and then finally we could use gfp  complementation with super resolution  microscopy  to investigate structural components in  c2  so i'd like to thank you for your time  and mostly i want to thank  um jim and virginia's towers for their  incredible generosity  um donating a large amount of their  wealth to make this towers institute's  mission possible  and to allow me to do the collaborative  work that i'm able to do  at the stowers institute thank you  wonderful talk um if anybody has  questions please put them  into the question box at the bottom of  your screen  uh the first question that we have is  what controls or counter screen  the incorporal or the lack of  reversibility of the gfp  by visc once formed  yeah that's a really good question um so  the the best c molecule that we're using  is one i i put the reference to the  cabin two's paper  in in that slide and so if you can look  back at the slides you can go see that  um but in in particular this what gfp of  molecule is reversible  um how reversible it is um depends on  time scale  and i believe the the dissociation  constant  is in the micromolar regime and um  so so we do think that we're probably  stabilizing  by a slight amount some interactions um  but the the correlation that we see with  the nano with the immunogold  and with the theta nuclei which don't  have this interaction on them  suggests to us that we're not  dramatically influencing  the fraction of a molecule of the  internuclear membrane  the second question is is split gfp  fluorescence  retained after fixation with  formaldehyde or other cross-linking  agents  yeah that's another great question  question um  i so i don't know how thoroughly we've  tested that  i know we saw some changes upon fixation  and so we ended up doing the entire  screen  with live cells but it would be  interesting to  explore that further especially for  higher throughput screening  a third question is how do you think the  structure of the nuclear pore complex  influences the composition of the  internuclear envelope  yeah so that's a really good question so  um if covet wouldn't have happened  i would be probably talking about that  today we've been using super resolution  with split gfp  to try to understand the the structure  of the nuclear pore complex  and and so we we don't have a complete  answer to that  um but we're definitely working on tools  to more thoroughly explore the structure  of the nuclear pore complex and try to  understand  how proteins use that to access the  inner nuclear membrane and to leave the  internet  membrane i have a question for you  so since this is a reversible  complex and you can see almost at a  single  cell resolution are you able to measure  residence times  of the protein protein interactions  we haven't done that but i think that's  a great idea  um it would be really cool to look at  these  um in mad deletions and see if that  changes the residence time  um so i think that's a great idea but we  haven't done it yet  okay we have time for just a couple more  questions um how would you apply your  technology to other cell types for  example mammalian cells  yeah so the struggle we have with  mammalian cells is that um  so we have this plus one molecule that  is  nicely nucleoplasmic and um basically  has  zero concentration in the cytoplasm and  so that allows us to interrogate only  the inner nuclear membrane  and in mammalian cells that's harder to  generate um  and so we're looking at a lot of  different options for trying to  understand  what goes on in mammalian cells but it's  an area of active investigation  great um there's a question of is gfp  fluorescence reconstitution here  happens due to two proteins  co-localizing  or colloidalizing and interacting as  well  yeah so this is interaction here but the  co-localization that i talked about when  i talked about fluorescence  cross-correlation spectroscopy that was  co-localization that was actually  tripartite  co-localization so it was the  complementation of the gfp  plus co-diffusion with another another  protein  that we were looking at thank you very  much  jay i want to thank you for a wonderful  presentation and everyone for listening  take care  thank you
 

